&lt;p&gt;D'après les données de sa page, OVHcloud a passé le .be à 2,99&amp;nbsp;€ HT et le .io à 17,49&amp;nbsp;€ HT du 17 novembre au 1er décembre 2026&amp;nbsp;; le .dev est en promotion jusqu'au 31 décembre. Hors .ma (132&amp;nbsp;€ puis 264&amp;nbsp;€ TTC chez Gandi, seul prix relevé), le .io est l'extension la plus chère du lot&amp;nbsp;: entre 59,79&amp;nbsp;€ HT (OVHcloud) et 77,99&amp;nbsp;€ TTC (Gandi) par an au renouvellement, 51.80&amp;nbsp;$ chez Porkbun.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  Qui peut enregistrer quoi : les règles des registres
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Le .fr et le .ca ne sont pas ouverts à tous&amp;nbsp;; le .be et le .ch le sont, et le .ma l'est selon la FAQ de son registre. Règles lues sur les sites des registres&amp;nbsp;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;.fr (Afnic).&lt;/strong&gt; «&amp;nbsp;Le .fr est ouvert aux personnes morales et aux personnes physiques résidant au sein de l'Union Européenne mais aussi en Suisse, Norvège, Islande et Liechtenstein&amp;nbsp;» (&lt;a href="https://www.afnic.fr/noms-de-domaine/tout-savoir/pourquoi-choisir-le-fr/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;page Afnic sur le .fr&lt;/a&gt;). Un résident du Maroc ou du Canada ne peut donc pas déposer un .fr à son nom.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;.be (DNS Belgium).&lt;/strong&gt; Les &lt;a href="https://assets.dnsbelgium.be/attachment/Enduser_Terms_and_Conditions_en_v6.2.pdf" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;conditions générales du .be&lt;/a&gt; (version 6.2, applicable depuis le 15 janvier 2023) ne posent aucune condition de résidence&amp;nbsp;: le nom va au premier demandeur, via un agent d'enregistrement.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;.ch (SWITCH).&lt;/strong&gt; «&amp;nbsp;Il n'est pas nécessaire d'être suisse ni d'avoir son domicile ou son siège de société en Suisse pour enregistrer un nom de domaine .ch&amp;nbsp;» (&lt;a href="https://www.nic.ch/fr/support/general/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;FAQ de nic.ch&lt;/a&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;.ca (CIRA).&lt;/strong&gt; La règle est fixée par la politique de CIRA «&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://www.cira.ca/en/resources/documents/domains/canadian-presence-requirements-registrants/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Canadian Presence Requirements for Registrants&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;». Sa page a refusé notre lecture (erreur 403) les 17 et 18 novembre&amp;nbsp;: texte non relu. En résumé de revendeur, la &lt;a href="https://www.hostinger.com/fr/tld/domaine-ca" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;page .ca d'Hostinger&lt;/a&gt; indique que l'extension est «&amp;nbsp;uniquement disponible pour les individus ou organisations situés au Canada&amp;nbsp;».&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;.ma (ANRT).&lt;/strong&gt; Selon la &lt;a href="https://www.registre.ma/ressources/faq" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;FAQ du registre .ma&lt;/a&gt;, géré pour l'ANRT, «&amp;nbsp;toute personne physique ou morale peut procéder, auprès d'un prestataire, à une demande d'enregistrement&amp;nbsp;», sous réserve de la décision de nommage.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  Nom de domaine gratuit avec un hébergement
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Un domaine «&amp;nbsp;offert&amp;nbsp;» est presque toujours gratuit un an seulement, puis renouvelé au prix normal. Conditions lues le 18 novembre 2026&amp;nbsp;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;o2switch.&lt;/strong&gt; Domaine «&amp;nbsp;offert à vie&amp;nbsp;», «&amp;nbsp;inclus dans l'offre Cloud. &amp;amp; Pro.&amp;nbsp;» «&amp;nbsp;tant que votre hébergement reste actif&amp;nbsp;», pour .fr, .com, .net, .org, .be, .eu et cinq extensions d'outre-mer (&lt;a href="https://www.o2switch.fr/nom-de-domaine/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;page o2switch&lt;/a&gt;). C'est la seule offre relevée sans limite à un an.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;OVHcloud.&lt;/strong&gt; Domaine inclus «&amp;nbsp;pour une période d'un an pour tout achat d'un hébergement web&amp;nbsp;», puis «&amp;nbsp;le renouvellement est facturé au prix annuel de l'extension&amp;nbsp;» (&lt;a href="https://www.ovhcloud.com/fr/domains/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;page domaines&lt;/a&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;LWS.&lt;/strong&gt; «&amp;nbsp;Nom de domaine gratuit pour la création et le transfert du domaine la 1ère année, puis renouvellement possible au prix annuel de l'extension&amp;nbsp;» (&lt;a href="https://www.lws.fr/nom-de-domaine-gratuit.php" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;page domaine gratuit&lt;/a&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Hostinger.&lt;/strong&gt; Les packs d'hébergement d'un an ou plus comprennent un domaine gratuit, «&amp;nbsp;sauf le pack Single&amp;nbsp;» (&lt;a href="https://www.hostinger.com/fr/hebergement-web" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;page hébergement web&lt;/a&gt;)&amp;nbsp;; il est gratuit «&amp;nbsp;pendant 1&amp;nbsp;an&amp;nbsp;».&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Squarespace.&lt;/strong&gt; Un domaine gratuit la première année avec un abonnement annuel, sur certaines extensions, puis renouvelé à 20&amp;nbsp;$ (&lt;a href="https://support.squarespace.com/hc/en-us/articles/205812208-Squarespace-domains-FAQ" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;FAQ&lt;/a&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Pour un serveur à vous, notre comparatif des &lt;a href="https://neurautech.com/vps-pas-cher/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;VPS pas cher&lt;/a&gt; et notre guide de l'&lt;a href="https://neurautech.com/hebergement-cloud-pour-developpeurs/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;hébergement cloud pour développeurs&lt;/a&gt; détaillent les options&amp;nbsp;; un domaine pris chez un registrar séparé se pointe vers n'importe lequel.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  Sécuriser son nom de domaine
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Trois réglages protègent un domaine, tous proposés par les registrars relevés&amp;nbsp;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Le renouvellement automatique&lt;/strong&gt;, avec une carte valide. Chez LWS, il se choisit dans l'espace client&amp;nbsp;; Hostinger envoie des rappels avant l'expiration.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Le verrou de transfert&lt;/strong&gt; (clientTransferProhibited, appelé RegistrarLock chez Hostinger) bloque un transfert non autorisé. Chez Cloudflare, les domaines sont «&amp;nbsp;locked by default&amp;nbsp;» selon sa &lt;a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/domains/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;page Registrar&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;; LWS et OVHcloud le proposent.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;La double authentification sur le compte du registrar&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;: c'est elle qui protège tout le reste. Notre guide de la &lt;a href="https://neurautech.com/double-authentification/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;double authentification&lt;/a&gt; explique comment choisir la méthode.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Rangez les identifiants dans un &lt;a href="https://neurautech.com/gestionnaire-de-mot-de-passe-gratuit/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;gestionnaire de mot de passe&lt;/a&gt; et méfiez-vous des faux e-mails de renouvellement, un classique du &lt;a href="https://neurautech.com/phishing-comment-reconnaitre-et-eviter-les-arnaques/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;phishing&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Une fois le domaine pointé vers votre serveur, notre tutoriel &lt;a href="https://neurautech.com/nginx-reverse-proxy-docker-compose/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;reverse proxy Nginx avec Docker Compose&lt;/a&gt; ajoute le HTTPS. Pour un site WordPress, suivez notre liste pour &lt;a href="https://neurautech.com/securiser-wordpress/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;sécuriser WordPress&lt;/a&gt;. Pour une vitrine personnelle, notre guide du &lt;a href="https://neurautech.com/portfolio-developpeur-comment-le-construire-2026/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;portfolio développeur&lt;/a&gt; explique pourquoi un domaine à votre nom compte.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  Ce que nous n'avons pas pu vérifier
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Les éléments suivants sont absents ou incertains&amp;nbsp;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Namecheap et CIRA.&lt;/strong&gt; Leurs pages ont refusé notre lecture automatique (erreur 403)&amp;nbsp;; nous ne l'avons pas contournée.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Les prix chargés par script.&lt;/strong&gt; Infomaniak, Cloudflare, Squarespace (hors FAQ) et les pages par extension d'IONOS n'affichent pas leurs prix sans navigateur ou sans recherche&amp;nbsp;: non relevés.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;L'offre LWS à 0,99&amp;nbsp;€&lt;/strong&gt; pour .com, .fr et .be&amp;nbsp;: le bandeau l'annonce sans base de TVA, le tableau ne la montre pas, et nous n'avons pas ouvert de panier.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Le renouvellement IONOS.&lt;/strong&gt; Nous supposons qu'il correspond au prix barré (11&amp;nbsp;€ pour le .fr, 15&amp;nbsp;€ pour le .com).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Le renouvellement Porkbun du .com.&lt;/strong&gt; Le total de 35.43&amp;nbsp;$ suppose qu'il reste à 11.81&amp;nbsp;$&amp;nbsp;; la page ne le donne pas séparément.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Les frais ICANN d'Hostinger&lt;/strong&gt; (0,17&amp;nbsp;€)&amp;nbsp;: inclus ou non dans le prix, la page ne le dit pas. ICANN facturait aux registrars 0,20&amp;nbsp;$ par domaine et par an sur l'exercice 2026 (&lt;a href="https://www.icann.org/en/announcements/details/icann-accredited-registrars-approve-registrar-level-fees-for-fiscal-year-2026-21-07-2025-en" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;annonce ICANN&lt;/a&gt;)&amp;nbsp;; le montant de l'exercice en cours n'est pas relevé.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Plusieurs cases, notamment&amp;nbsp;:&lt;/strong&gt; transfert chez IONOS, Hostinger et Porkbun&amp;nbsp;; confidentialité WHOIS chez Gandi et IONOS&amp;nbsp;; .be chez Hostinger.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  Questions fréquentes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Quel est le nom de domaine pas cher le plus intéressant sur 3 ans ?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Pour un .fr, OVHcloud&amp;nbsp;: 20,57&amp;nbsp;€ HT sur 3&amp;nbsp;ans (4,99&amp;nbsp;€ puis 7,79&amp;nbsp;€ HT par an), d'après sa &lt;a href="https://www.ovhcloud.com/fr/domains/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;page domaines&lt;/a&gt; au 18 novembre 2026. Pour un .com, IONOS arrive à 31&amp;nbsp;€ HT si le renouvellement suit le prix barré de 15&amp;nbsp;€, devant Hostinger, LWS et OVHcloud (jusqu'à 37,07&amp;nbsp;€ HT, notre calcul).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  L'offre LWS à 0,99 € par an est-elle réelle ?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Le bandeau l'annonce pour .com, .fr, .be et .site, sans base de TVA, jusqu'au 21 novembre 2026 et pour les 1&amp;nbsp;000 premiers domaines. Le tableau ne l'affiche que pour .site, .online, .space, .website et .fun (0,99&amp;nbsp;€ HT), le .com restant à 6,99&amp;nbsp;€ HT&amp;nbsp;; remise non vérifiée au panier.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Combien coûte un nom de domaine .fr ?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Entre 0,01&amp;nbsp;€ HT (Hostinger) et 7,20&amp;nbsp;€ TTC (Gandi) la première année chez les registrars relevés, puis entre 7,79&amp;nbsp;€ HT (OVHcloud) et 28,78&amp;nbsp;€ TTC (Gandi) par an au renouvellement. Il faut résider dans l'Union européenne, en Suisse, en Norvège, en Islande ou au Liechtenstein.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Que veut dire « at cost » chez Cloudflare ?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Cloudflare revend le domaine au prix du registre et de l'ICANN, sans marge, mais impose ses serveurs de noms et ne rembourse pas les frais, selon sa &lt;a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/registrar/faq/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;FAQ&lt;/a&gt;. Le .fr n'y est pas proposé.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  À propos de l'auteur
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Abdelilah Ourti&lt;/strong&gt; est ingénieur IA et architecte logiciel. Il conçoit et déploie des applications web et des systèmes d'IA, et publie sur NeurauTech des guides et des comparatifs destinés aux développeurs francophones. Ses autres articles sont réunis sur &lt;a href="https://neurautech.com/author/neurautech/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;sa page auteur&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Méthode.&lt;/strong&gt; Synthèse fondée exclusivement sur les pages de tarifs officielles des registrars et sur les sites des registres (Afnic, DNS Belgium, SWITCH, registre .ma&amp;nbsp;; la politique de CIRA est citée mais sa page n'a pas pu être relue) et d'ICANN, lus le 17 novembre et relus le 18 novembre 2026. Les pages ont été lues dans leur version française quand elle existe et anglaise sinon (Porkbun, Cloudflare, Squarespace). Gandi affiche ses prix TTC pour la France&amp;nbsp;; les autres registrars européens, HT.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Calculs et indépendance.&lt;/strong&gt; Les prix sont repris tels qu'affichés, sans conversion de devise&amp;nbsp;; les totaux sur 3&amp;nbsp;ans et les équivalents HT ou TTC sont les nôtres. Aucun domaine n'a été acheté, aucun compte créé, aucun lien n'est affilié et aucune relation commerciale ne lie l'auteur aux registrars mentionnés.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Une erreur ou un prix périmé&amp;nbsp;?&lt;/strong&gt; Écrivez à &lt;a href="mailto:contact@neurautech.com"&gt;contact@neurautech.com&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;: l'article sera corrigé et la date de mise à jour modifiée.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Dernière mise à jour&amp;nbsp;: 18 novembre 2026.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Image&amp;nbsp;: Robbin Grimm (Unsplash License).&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A test only reports that something did not happen. The cause can sit in four places that have nothing to do with your code:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;the page was slow and the test ran out of time;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;the host could not be reached, for example because the name did not resolve;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;the site defended itself with a 403, a 429, a CAPTCHA or a bot challenge, so the request never reached your product;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;the browser or the process died.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When every one of these reads as a plain red, the team files them as bugs, or learns to rerun reds instead of reading them. Either way the real defect hides in the noise.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Disclosure: I run &lt;a href="https://pickaplugin.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;PickAPlugin&lt;/a&gt;, an independent catalog of ChatGPT and Codex plugins (so it's on this list). This is adapted from our guide on the same topic.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You built an MCP server. Now people need to find it. Every place below has a free option, and I'd do them in this order.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  1. Official MCP Registry (do this first)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;registry.modelcontextprotocol.io is run by the MCP project, and other directories and clients pull from it. Add a &lt;code&gt;server.json&lt;/code&gt;, log in (GitHub or DNS proves your namespace), then run &lt;code&gt;mcp-publisher publish&lt;/code&gt;. Your package (npm, PyPI, Docker...) has to exist first.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  2. awesome-mcp-servers (GitHub PR)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open a PR to &lt;code&gt;punkpeye/awesome-mcp-servers&lt;/code&gt; adding one line in the right category, in alphabetical order. It's for servers with a public repo; hosted-only servers go to awesome-remote-mcp-servers. It also feeds Glama.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  3. The free submit forms
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;MCP Market&lt;/strong&gt; (mcpmarket.com/submit): free, or $29 one-time to skip the queue.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;mcpservers.org&lt;/strong&gt; (/submit): free review takes up to two weeks; $39 for a 24-hour review.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;mcp.so&lt;/strong&gt; (/submit): free, or $39 for instant publish with a verified badge.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Smithery&lt;/strong&gt; (smithery.ai/new): point it at a repo or hosted URL.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;LobeHub MCP&lt;/strong&gt; (lobehub.com/mcp): submit button with your repo.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Glama&lt;/strong&gt; (glama.ai/mcp/servers): "Add Server", with quality and license scores.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;cursor.directory&lt;/strong&gt;: good if your server targets Cursor users.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;PickAPlugin&lt;/strong&gt;: free listing, email the repo link via the submit page.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  4. Docker MCP Catalog
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you ship a container, add an entry by PR to &lt;code&gt;docker/mcp-registry&lt;/code&gt;. Local servers need a Dockerfile in the repo.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Before you submit
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Make sure your README has a one-line description and a copy-paste install command. Showing both JSON and TOML configs helps, since Claude and Codex use different formats.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You don't need the paid options to get listed. The full guide, with the sourced traffic numbers and an FAQ, is at &lt;a href="https://pickaplugin.com/guides/where-to-submit-mcp-server" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://pickaplugin.com/guides/where-to-submit-mcp-server&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When I started building GPCB Pay, I thought displaying the card payment interface in WooCommerce would be the difficult part.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I was wrong. 😅&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The bigger challenge was everything happening &lt;strong&gt;after a customer clicked Pay&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What happens when PayPal approves an interaction but the capture hasn't been verified? What if a network request times out? What if the same capture request is triggered twice?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These questions led me to build &lt;strong&gt;GPCB Pay (General PayPal Card Bridge)&lt;/strong&gt;, an independently developed WooCommerce payment integration.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I recently released its open-source WordPress connector in the official WordPress Plugin Directory. I wanted to share the architecture behind it and some lessons from building a payment SaaS independently.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  1. A Payment Button Is Not a Payment System
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Integrating a payment provider is more than displaying a checkout button or collecting a successful browser callback.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I designed GPCB Pay around three important rules:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Browser approval is not proof of payment.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Creating a PayPal Order does not mean the customer has paid.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;WooCommerce should complete an order only after successful server-verified capture.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That distinction matters.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A customer can approve a payment while the server is still processing the financial operation. Network interruptions and repeated requests can also leave the application uncertain about the outcome.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The system needs to handle those situations without assuming that a payment succeeded or blindly repeating a capture.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  2. The Architecture: Three Separate Components
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of placing the entire payment system inside a WordPress plugin, I separated GPCB Pay into three components.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  WooCommerce Connector
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A lightweight WordPress plugin that integrates with WooCommerce Checkout Blocks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It handles gateway configuration, checkout integration, authenticated communication with the backend, and WooCommerce order operations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It isn't intended to store raw card numbers, CVV, or merchant PayPal Client Secrets.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Private SaaS Payment Core
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A Node.js and TypeScript backend responsible for sensitive payment operations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Its responsibilities include:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Creating PayPal Orders.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Coordinating and verifying Captures.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Idempotency and duplicate-capture safeguards.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Durable payment records and recovery.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Merchant and store authorization.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Subscription access enforcement.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The server coordinates financial operations rather than trusting the browser to declare an order paid.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Merchant Dashboard
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A React-based application where merchants can manage onboarding and payment configuration.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This includes store provisioning, domain verification, Connector credentials, PayPal Sandbox and Live connections, and service-access status.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Separating these responsibilities makes the payment boundaries clearer and allows the WooCommerce Connector to remain relatively small.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  3. Why Server-Verified Capture Matters
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Imagine a customer approves a payment, but the next network request times out.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Did the payment fail? Did PayPal complete the Capture before the connection disappeared?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The browser alone cannot reliably answer those questions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In GPCB Pay, the backend is responsible for verifying the provider's Capture outcome before WooCommerce can complete the order.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are several safeguards around that process.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Idempotency:&lt;/strong&gt; Repeated requests associated with the same payment attempt should not automatically create repeated financial effects.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Duplicate-Capture protection:&lt;/strong&gt; The system must prevent an already captured payment from being captured again.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Durable state:&lt;/strong&gt; Payment evidence must survive a request ending or an application restart.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Recovery and reconciliation:&lt;/strong&gt; When an operation has an uncertain outcome, the original attempt needs verification before another financial action is considered.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One of the biggest lessons I learned is that handling an uncertain outcome is just as important as handling a successful one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  4. Guest Card vs. Advanced Card
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;GPCB Pay supports two explicitly configured payment experiences through eligible PayPal services.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Guest Card&lt;/strong&gt; can provide a debit or credit card payment option through PayPal, depending on the merchant and checkout context.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Advanced Card&lt;/strong&gt; uses PayPal-hosted card fields within the WooCommerce checkout where the merchant has the required capabilities and approval.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Both experiences depend on PayPal eligibility, supported countries, merchant configuration, and checkout conditions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Connecting PayPal successfully does not guarantee that every card payment option will be available.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Version 0.1.0 is designed for &lt;strong&gt;WooCommerce Checkout Blocks&lt;/strong&gt;, not Classic Checkout. Automatic switching between Guest and Advanced modes is not implemented.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  5. Merchant Onboarding Is Also a Security Problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Another challenge was connecting the correct WooCommerce website to the correct merchant account.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The setup process includes:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Merchant registration and email verification.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Creating a WooCommerce store in the Merchant Dashboard.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Issuing a Connector Key ID and Signing Secret.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Verifying domain ownership using a DNS TXT record.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Proving Connector credential possession through an authenticated request.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Completing the authorized store activation workflow.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Configuring PayPal Sandbox and Live credentials.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Testing connectivity and confirming payment readiness.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These checks serve different purposes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;DNS verification proves domain control. Connector proof demonstrates possession of the configured credentials. Neither one, by itself, authorizes payment processing.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This separation helped me think more carefully about trust boundaries throughout the application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  6. Full WooCommerce Setup Tutorial
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've also published an English tutorial showing the actual GPCB Pay interfaces and onboarding process.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It covers plugin installation, merchant registration, DNS verification, Connector configuration, PayPal Sandbox and Live setup, subscription access, and the eligible card checkout experiences.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;  &lt;iframe src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/NWdg48OdxGM" width="710" height="399"&gt;
  &lt;/iframe&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The video is based on real product interfaces. Actual payment-method availability can differ depending on each merchant's PayPal eligibility.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  7. The Project Is Now Available on WordPress.org
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;GPCB Pay's WooCommerce Connector is free and open-source under GPLv2 or later.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The operational SaaS Payment Core is a separate external service with an eligible trial or paid subscription. PayPal processing fees are separate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you're interested in exploring the project:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;WordPress Plugin:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://wordpress.org/plugins/gpcb-pay-card-payments-for-woocommerce/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Official Website:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://gpcbpay.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Setup Documentation:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://gpcbpay.com/documentation/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;GPCB Pay is independently developed and is not affiliated with, sponsored by, or endorsed by PayPal or WooCommerce.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  8. What I Learned Building This Independently
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Building a payment integration pushed me to think beyond the user interface.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A successful checkout isn't just a nice-looking payment form. It needs verified financial outcomes, carefully managed authorization boundaries, persistent state, and deliberate recovery behavior.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm still learning and improving the engineering behind the project, and sharing that process is part of why I built it publicly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;For developers who have worked with WooCommerce or payment APIs: what's the most difficult payment edge case you've encountered?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'd genuinely like to hear your experiences and feedback.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Flo3poa71efngflzz32aw.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Flo3poa71efngflzz32aw.png" alt=" " width="800" height="420"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;TIMEOUT: the test never got an answer in time. That is not proof the application is broken, and it is not proof it works: the run cannot tell. A navigation that ran out of time is a TIMEOUT, because it started and the page was slow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;UNREACHABLE: the host could not be reached at all. DNS or the connection failed before any page was observed, as with a name that did not resolve, a refused connection or an offline machine. A certificate error is not here: that one is a finding.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;BLOCKED: the site defended itself with a 403, a 429, a block page or a bot challenge. When a defense was on screen, metadata.json names it. A real Android phone that fell asleep or is locked is BLOCKED too, as an unmet prerequisite.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;CRASHED: the browser or the process died, for example a closed page, a DevTools protocol error or a dead WebDriver session. It is infrastructure: look at the machine and the driver.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The verdicts guide lists the exact error patterns behind each one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;By the end of this tutorial you will have a Python script that pulls Belgian property listings from immoweb.be into structured rows: asking price, address, living area, land area, bedrooms, EPC label, agency and photo links.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Immoweb is the Belgian real-estate portal. &lt;strong&gt;Immoweb Scraper&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;code&gt;reventadata/immoweb-scraper&lt;/code&gt;) is the Apify Actor that reads its public listings. You describe a search with a few inputs, and it returns one row per listing. It is not affiliated with Immoweb.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This guide runs the Actor from Python with the Apify API client. The sample rows below come from a real run of a postal code 9000 search (Gent), copied from its dataset, not from an invented example.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  Prerequisites
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You need three things:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Python 3.11 or newer&lt;/strong&gt;. The Apify client for Python 3.x requires it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A free &lt;strong&gt;Apify account&lt;/strong&gt;, created with an email address or a GitHub login.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Your &lt;strong&gt;API token&lt;/strong&gt;, from &lt;strong&gt;Settings &amp;gt; API &amp;amp; Integrations&lt;/strong&gt; in Apify Console.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Install the client and export the token:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;pip install -U &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"apify-client~=3.0"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;APIFY_API_TOKEN&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"your_apify_token_here"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;No scraping code is needed on your side. The Actor reads public pages, with no login, and does the fetching.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  What you'll achieve
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You will search Gent (postal code 9000) for houses for sale, read the rows in Python and print a summary line per listing. The summary includes a price per square metre that you work out yourself from two fields. The example run finished in 4.2 seconds.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same script covers a rental search, another postal code or a price ceiling. Only the input changes. Each run takes one &lt;code&gt;propertyType&lt;/code&gt; and one &lt;code&gt;transaction&lt;/code&gt;, so a mixed search of houses and apartments needs one run per type.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 1: Open Immoweb Scraper
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open the &lt;a href="https://apify.com/reventadata/immoweb-scraper" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Immoweb Scraper page on Apify Store&lt;/a&gt;. The &lt;strong&gt;Try for free&lt;/strong&gt; button there opens the Actor in Apify Console. This tutorial calls the Actor from the API, so you only need its name, &lt;code&gt;reventadata/immoweb-scraper&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 2: Choose the search
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The search is described by these input fields:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;propertyType&lt;/code&gt;: &lt;code&gt;house&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;apartment&lt;/code&gt;. Any other value is refused before the run starts.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;transaction&lt;/code&gt;: &lt;code&gt;buy&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;rent&lt;/code&gt;. On a rent search, &lt;code&gt;price&lt;/code&gt; is the monthly rent.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;postalCodes&lt;/code&gt;: a list of four-digit Belgian postal codes such as &lt;code&gt;9000&lt;/code&gt; (Gent) or &lt;code&gt;1000&lt;/code&gt; (Brussels). Each code is one search, run in the order given. An empty list searches all of Belgium in one search.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;minPrice&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;maxPrice&lt;/code&gt;: optional limits in EUR. An empty field means no limit. On a rent search they limit the monthly rent.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;maxItems&lt;/code&gt;: a cap on rows across all searches. The default is 1000.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;maxPages&lt;/code&gt;: an optional cap on result pages per search, with 60 listings on each page.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;fetchDetails&lt;/code&gt;: off by default. It adds the agency's public contact fields (see Step 5).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Immoweb pages any one search to at most 9,960 listings (166 pages of 60). A larger search is reported in the run log, and splitting it by postal code or by a price band reaches the rest.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To search two postal codes for apartments to rent, the input would look like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"propertyType"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"apartment"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"transaction"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"rent"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"postalCodes"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"9000"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"1000"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"maxPrice"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1200&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"maxItems"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;50&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"maxPages"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;






&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 3: Run it from Python
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This script starts the Actor with a search for houses for sale in postal code 9000, waits for it to finish, reads the dataset and prints two lines per listing. The first two input values are the Actor's defaults, so they could be left out.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;apify_client&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ApifyClient&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# 1. Initialise the client with your token from the environment&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ApifyClient&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;environ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"APIFY_API_TOKEN"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# 2. Describe the search: houses for sale in postal code 9000, ten rows&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;run_input&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"propertyType"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"house"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"transaction"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"buy"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"postalCodes"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"9000"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"maxItems"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"maxPages"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# 3. Start the Actor and wait for it to finish&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;actor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"reventadata/immoweb-scraper"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;call&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;run_input&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;run_input&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;is&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;None&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;or&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"SUCCEEDED"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"unknown"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;raise&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;SystemExit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Run did not succeed (status: {status})."&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# 4. Read the rows from the run's default dataset&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;items&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;dataset&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;default_dataset_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;list_items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;items&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Retrieved {len(items)} listings.\n"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# 5. Print two lines per listing, with a price per square metre worked out here&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;street&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;" "&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;part&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;part&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"street"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"houseNumber"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;part&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;price&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;area&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"price"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"livingAreaM2"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;per_m2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"{price / area:,.0f} EUR/m2"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;price&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;and&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;area&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"no living area"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"{street}, {item.get('locality')} | {price} EUR | {area} m2 | {per_m2}"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"  {item.get('propertySubtype')}, bedrooms: {item.get('bedrooms')}, EPC: {item.get('epcLabel')}"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The run reads its results page by page, so keep &lt;code&gt;maxPages&lt;/code&gt; small while you test.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 4: Read the output
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The loop prints one block per listing. Here are the first three listings of the example run's ten rows:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Sint-Denijslaan 359, Gent | 949000 EUR | 188 m2 | 5,048 EUR/m2
  VILLA, bedrooms: 3, EPC: C
Wijmenstraat 16, Gent | 248000 EUR | 162 m2 | 1,531 EUR/m2
  HOUSE, bedrooms: 3, EPC: F
Koningin Elisabethlaan 76, Gent | 1900000 EUR | 558 m2 | 3,405 EUR/m2
  HOUSE, bedrooms: 3, EPC: B
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Each row carries the full set of fields. One complete row from the same run, the second listing, follows. &lt;code&gt;imageUrls&lt;/code&gt; is cut to its first entry, and &lt;code&gt;agencyLogoUrl&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;searchUrl&lt;/code&gt; are left out.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"21993768"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"url"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://www.immoweb.be/en/classified/house/for-sale/gent/9000/21993768"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"title"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Home with an open-plan feel and a large garden"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"propertyType"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"HOUSE"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"propertySubtype"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"HOUSE"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"transaction"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"FOR_SALE"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"price"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;248000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"currency"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"EUR"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"priceLabel"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"€248,000"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"priceKind"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"residential_sale"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"priceMin"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
